/*
:name: mem_fwd_selfwrite
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: mem_fwd_selfwrite.rf
:pyrope_top: rf
:state_pair_gap: mems=0/1
*/

// MEMORY vs FLOP-ARRAY+MUX, with a FORWARDING read of a SELF-WRITTEN array.
//
// The Pyrope side is a Memory cell; the Verilog reference (.v) is the same
// machine built from FOUR NAMED FLOPS and explicit read/write muxes, with no
// array at all. Pairing a memory against flop+mux logic is the register-file
// correspondence that matters in real designs, and it is why this is not a
// memory-vs-memory self-LEC (that would discharge by structural identity and
// check nothing about forwarding).
//
// Two properties have to hold together, and their combination is what is hard:
//
//   * the array is written from a FUNCTION OF ITSELF (`t[wsel] = t[wsel] | a`),
//     so the write-data cone reads the array's own dout;
//   * the read is FORWARDING at a RUNTIME index (`z = t[rsel]`, with the write
//     above it in the same cycle), so the dout is a select of the NEXT-state
//     array rather than of the committed one.
//
// That pair is the shape that kept minion's `intpipe_csr_file` UNKNOWN (a
// variable-index read of an array's NEXT comb value).
//
// The explicit `rst` arm is NOT decoration. Without it the memory carries only
// power-on `init` while the flops carry their own, the state correspondence
// cannot pair one array against four flops (`ref{t0..t3} impl{t}` stays
// unpaired), and the miter refutes on the FREE INITIAL VALUE instead of on the
// forwarding logic -- measured. Clearing every entry on both sides makes the
// post-reset state concrete, so the comparison is about behaviour.
pub mod rf(rst:bool, a:u8, wsel:u2, rsel:u2, we:bool) -> (z:u8@[0]) {
  reg t:[4]u8
  if rst {
    t[0] = 0
    t[1] = 0
    t[2] = 0
    t[3] = 0
  } elif we {
    t[wsel] = t[wsel] | a   // self-referential write: the wdata cone reads the array
  }
  z = t[rsel]               // forwarding read at a RUNTIME index: sees the write above
}
